      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-341216",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform \n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}\u20614t}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>\\omega .\n$$\n\nLet $f(t)=\\operatorname{cosh}\u20614t$ and $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={{field-2}},\\,s>4.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{\u2033}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}\u20614t}={{field-4}},\\,s>4.\n$$"
    },
